<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kamehameha III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1854">1854</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Kauikeaouli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kamehameha III was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> from 1825 to 1854, the longest reign of the monarchy. He issued the kingdom's first constitution in 1840 and the Great Māhele of 1848, which introduced private land ownership and allowed foreigners to buy land. He won recognition of Hawaiian independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1843.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii.jpg" alt="Kamehameha III" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hugo Stangenwald · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photo_of_Kamehameha_III_(PP-97-7-003).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Kamehameha III</b> (r. 1825–1854), advised by former missionaries such as William Richards and Gerrit P. Judd, the kingdom became a constitutional monarchy: a declaration of rights in 1839 was followed by the constitution of 1840, which created a House of Nobles, an elected House of Representatives and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Kamehameha III</b> established the first Hawaiian-language constitution in 1839 and 1840, and in 1840, taking over the mission’s schools, the legislature made state-funded education compulsory for all children under 14, girls included, twelve years before any such law in the United States.</blockquote><a href="/language/hawaiian">Hawaiian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Keauhou Bay, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.56,-155.96&amp;year=1839" aria-label="Keauhou Bay on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="338 1439 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kamehameha III"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M788 1709h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M788 1709h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.56,-155.96&amp;year=1839">What was here in 1839? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
